// ── THE ALLOWLIST ASSERTION, IN THE EXECUTION PATH ───────────────────────────
//
// FIRST statement in the file, deliberately, and the only import it has. This
// module asserts — before this file's body runs, before any client is
// constructed and before any query is issued — which project this process is
// pointed at, and exits 2 if it cannot establish it.
//
// It is not a workflow step, so no YAML edit can skip it. Deleting the
// `Preflight — Supabase target must be the sanctioned CI project` step from
// .github/workflows/live-db.yml, disabling it with `if:`, moving it after the
// install step, wrapping it in a shell conditional, or adding a brand-new job
// in a brand-new workflow file all still land here, because this process
// cannot start without it. That is what replaced five rounds of trying to
// prove the YAML step was present and real.
//
// This is the READ-ONLY front door, not src/lib/ciSupabaseGuard.mjs. In CI it
// behaves identically — the sanctioned CI project, or exit 2. Outside CI, and
// only outside CI, it additionally permits a read-only audit of the declared
// production project when the operator asks for it by name:
//
//   PORTAVA_PROD_READ_ONLY_AUDIT='read-only-audit-against-production'
//
// This script qualifies because both of its statements are SELECTs — the
// dangling-row join over post_media and storage.objects, and the orphan count.
// It reconciles rows against the bucket and changes neither; the remedy it
// prints is for a human to choose. Production is where the 114 broken images
// were found. If it ever gains a write, move it back to
// src/lib/ciSupabaseGuard.mjs and drop it from the read-only list in
// scripts/check-guard-coverage.mjs, which enforces that list on every run.
//
// See src/lib/ciProdReadOnlyAuditGuard.mjs and docs/ci/README.md.
import "../lib/ciProdReadOnlyAuditGuard.mjs";

